{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Do you mean to kill me?</b> From here we learn that he killed him with the explicit Name — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 2:14), and it is the midrash of our Sages (Shemot Rabbah 1:35). And I am astonished: if so, who told the wicked man that Moses had killed him? Perhaps he laid his hand upon him and cursed him with the Name of YHWH, and this is the meaning of \"and he smote\" (וַיַּךְ). Or because a dead man fell before him, Moses feared they would frame him and buried him in the sand, and the wicked man saw him doing so and knew that he was the cause, or he supposed that he had killed him with the sword, for he saw only the burial. And according to the plain sense, the commentators say (the commentators, Ibn Ezra here and Radak in the Book of Roots, root אמר) that <b>you say</b> means \"you think,\" for we find אֲמִירָה used for the thought of the heart: \"I said in my heart\" (Ecclesiastes 2:1), \"I said, better than him is the fallen one\" (Ecclesiastes 6:3). But there is no need for this, for he said <b>Who made you a prince and a judge over us?</b> — do you mean to kill me <b>as you killed the Egyptian</b>? If it is because you desire to kill me, do you rebuke me and say, \"Why do you strike your fellow?\""], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:01:08.066201641Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}